{"t":"panel","id":"gear-principle","title":"Why seven gears?","body":"One chainring times seven rear sprockets gives seven ratios. A larger rear sprocket makes climbing easier; a smaller one moves the bike farther per pedal turn. The derailleur guides the chain sideways, while its sprung cage takes up slack.","anchor":"drivetrain"}
{"t":"panel","id":"design-tradeoff","title":"Simple, practical layout","body":"This example uses a 1×7 derailleur drivetrain and rim brakes; seven-speed hub-geared bikes use a different mechanism. Sprocket teeth, chain links and most spokes are simplified here to keep the layout readable.","anchor":"frame"}
{"t":"controls","id":"bike-controls","buttons":[{"label":"Explode","action":{"op":"explode","amount":1}},{"label":"Assemble","action":{"op":"explode","amount":0}},{"label":"Show seven gears","action":{"op":"focus","system":"drivetrain"}},{"label":"Reset","action":{"op":"reset"}}]}
{"t":"say","text":"The frame carries you, the fork steers, and the brakes slow the rims. The seven rear sprockets let you trade pedalling effort for distance travelled, without needing a front derailleur."}
{"t":"done"}
